To sort addresses, turn each into a number. `std::uintptr_t` (from `<cstdint>`) is an unsigned integer big enough to hold an address: `reinterpret_cast<std::uintptr_t>(&local)`. Keep a small struct per entry and sort with a lambda:

```cpp
struct Entry { std::uintptr_t address; std::string name; };
std::vector<Entry> entries{ /* ... */ };
std::sort(entries.begin(), entries.end(),
          [](const Entry& a, const Entry& b) { return a.address < b.address; });
```
